WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:08.874
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:15.704
Unit twenty-eight: Social Entrepreneurship. Today you will combine aims with both … and / either … or.

4
00:00:16.004 --> 00:00:18.691
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.646
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:25.262
Social enterprise.

7
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:31.988
Social enterprise means a business that trades to achieve a social or environmental mission.

8
00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:36.313
The social enterprise trains former street children as chefs.

9
00:00:36.917 --> 00:00:39.906
Your turn. Say: Social enterprise.

10
00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:44.580
Social enterprise.

11
00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:46.178
Mission.

12
00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:49.938
Mission means an organisation's core purpose.

13
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.281
Their mission is to give rural women a steady income.

14
00:00:53.883 --> 00:00:56.257
Your turn. Say: Mission.

15
00:00:59.100 --> 00:00:59.904
Mission.

16
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:01.698
Impact.

17
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:06.239
Impact means the positive change an organisation creates.

18
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.848
They measure impact by the number of graduates employed.

19
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.882
Your turn. Say: Impact.

20
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:16.746
Impact.

21
00:01:17.483 --> 00:01:18.252
Profit.

22
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.923
Profit means money earned after costs are paid.

23
00:01:22.517 --> 00:01:25.143
Profit is reinvested in training programmes.

24
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.623
Your turn. Say: Profit.

25
00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:32.305
Profit.

26
00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.098
Community.

27
00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:39.324
Community means a group of people living in the same place or sharing interests.

28
00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:43.170
The whole community benefits from the clean water project.

29
00:01:43.783 --> 00:01:46.679
Your turn. Say: Community.

30
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:50.511
Community.

31
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:52.576
Sustainability.

32
00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:57.499
Sustainability means the ability to continue over a long period.

33
00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:01.511
Selling products gives the project financial sustainability.

34
00:02:02.117 --> 00:02:05.582
Your turn. Say: Sustainability.

35
00:02:08.433 --> 00:02:09.829
Sustainability.

36
00:02:10.583 --> 00:02:11.712
Innovation.

37
00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:15.204
Innovation means a creative new solution.

38
00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:18.571
Their key innovation was recycled-tyre sandals.

39
00:02:19.183 --> 00:02:21.940
Your turn. Say: Innovation.

40
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:25.894
Innovation.

41
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:27.790
Beneficiary.

42
00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:32.118
Beneficiary means a person who receives help or benefit.

43
00:02:32.717 --> 00:02:35.587
Each beneficiary receives six months of training.

44
00:02:36.183 --> 00:02:38.638
Your turn. Say: Beneficiary.

45
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:42.640
Beneficiary.

46
00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:47.316
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:53.809
Visal, the training restaurant in Siem Reap serves excellent food. Is it a charity or a business?

48
00:02:54.917 --> 00:03:04.163
It's both a business and a social mission. Tourists pay normal prices, and the profit funds training for disadvantaged young people.

49
00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.644
Why not simply rely on donations?

50
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:15.919
Donations can stop at any time. Trading gives both independence and long-term sustainability.

51
00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:19.411
Do they ever face difficult choices?

52
00:03:20.517 --> 00:03:27.674
Often. They must either raise prices or train fewer students, so measuring impact helps them decide.

53
00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:30.461
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.317 --> 00:03:34.617
Grammar focus: Both and and either or.

55
00:03:34.917 --> 00:03:36.231
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.917 --> 00:03:39.822
Social enterprises seek both profit and impact.

57
00:03:40.717 --> 00:03:44.005
They must either raise prices or train fewer students.

58
00:03:44.917 --> 00:03:48.275
Trading provides both independence and sustainability.

59
00:03:49.233 --> 00:03:58.796
'Both … and' adds two things together and takes a plural verb: Both profit and impact matter. 'Either … or' offers a choice. Keep both parts grammatically parallel.

60
00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.141
Be careful! Don't say: They seek both profit or impact.

61
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.657
Say: They seek both profit and impact.

62
00:04:08.957 --> 00:04:09.958
Both … and.

63
00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:13.303
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.007
Doing Well by Doing Good.

65
00:04:16.307 --> 00:04:21.952
Social entrepreneurship combines the problem-solving energy of business with the purpose of a charity.

66
00:04:22.967 --> 00:04:30.841
Unlike traditional charities, social enterprises earn revenue by selling goods or services, which reduces dependence on donors.

67
00:04:31.833 --> 00:04:39.440
Cambodia has become known for such enterprises, from restaurants training vulnerable youth to workshops employing people with disabilities.

68
00:04:40.433 --> 00:04:47.251
These organisations constantly balance commercial pressure with their mission, and either goal can suffer if the other dominates.

69
00:04:48.267 --> 00:04:54.272
Success is measured both by financial results and by the lasting change experienced by beneficiaries.

70
00:04:55.367 --> 00:04:59.763
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:00.617 --> 00:05:03.452
The project helps blank farmers and their families.

72
00:05:03.752 --> 00:05:06.709
A, either. B, both. C, neither.

73
00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:12.208
The answer is B: both.

74
00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.953
A person who receives help or benefit is a blank.

75
00:05:16.253 --> 00:05:20.243
A, beneficiary. B, mission. C, community.

76
00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.985
The answer is A: beneficiary.

77
00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:29.753
An organisation's core purpose is its blank.

78
00:05:30.053 --> 00:05:33.718
A, profit. B, innovation. C, mission.

79
00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:38.848
The answer is C: mission.

80
00:05:39.983 --> 00:05:41.751
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:42.051 --> 00:05:49.106
A, Both price or quality matter.. B, Both price and quality matter.. C, Both price and quality matters..

82
00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:55.665
The answer is B: Both price and quality matter..

83
00:05:56.817 --> 00:06:01.080
According to the reading, social enterprises reduce dependence on donors by…

84
00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:05.730
A, earning revenue. B, cutting staff. C, avoiding profit.

85
00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:11.649
The answer is A: earning revenue.

86
00:06:12.833 --> 00:06:16.101
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:19.658
What social problem could a business help solve?

88
00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:37.823
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:38.123 --> 00:07:13.218
A business could help solve youth unemployment, which affects many young Cambodians who lack practical skills. For example, a social enterprise café could both serve customers and train young people from poor families in cooking, service and English. Its profits would pay for each trainee's course, while graduates would gain employment in hotels. In my view, businesses can sometimes solve problems more sustainably than charities, because they generate their own funding.

90
00:07:13.518 --> 00:07:15.402
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:16.317 --> 00:07:20.641
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and both and and either or.

92
00:07:20.941 --> 00:07:26.681
Today's value is social impact. Use your skills and ideas to improve other people's lives.

93
00:07:26.981 --> 00:07:29.820
See you in Unit twenty-nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.424
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

